This layer declares itself runnable by CI on a stock GitHub runner.

Presence of this file is the declaration; the CI job globs for it and never
names a layer. `image:` lines are data the job reads — every image this
layer's smoke test runs, pulled with retries before verify so a rate-limited
registry skips this layer loudly instead of reddening the gate. Everything
else here is prose for humans.

image: alpine:3
image: nginx:alpine

What declaring it promises (contract: ../README.md):

- `setup.sh` is a no-op wherever dockerd is already running, and the runner
  image runs dockerd. Provisioning downloads nothing.
- `smoke-test.sh` needs no sandbox-only facility. It degrades on its own
  where the egress proxy's CA bundle is absent, which is the runner's case.

Known soft spot, deliberately not hidden: the container-egress check fetches
a file from `raw.githubusercontent.com`, and that is a hard failure inside
the smoke test, so an outage there reds this job. The `image:` softening
cannot reach it — a precondition inside the test is the test's own business,
and changing what a smoke test checks belongs to the layer's own work.

What it does NOT promise: that a green run here substitutes for
`./joharness.sh verify` on the layer a repo actually selects. It is coverage
for THIS layer, nothing wider — and a run that skipped this layer proves
nothing at all, which is why step 7 says read the run, never the marker.
